{
    "attributes": {
        "action": "Modernise the law on mental health and criminal responsibility",
        "additional_details": "The current insanity rules date from 1843 and lag behind modern psychiatric understanding. Although prosecutors can consider mental health when deciding whether prosecution is in the public interest, we want a stronger statutory framework requiring relevant expert evidence to be considered before prosecution and, where a case proceeds, when determining criminal responsibility, not only at sentencing. Diagnosis alone should not be an automatic defence.",
        "background": "Review and reform the law on criminal responsibility in England and Wales so courts can consider whether a recognised mental, developmental, neurological or personality disorder substantially impaired a defendant\u2019s rational judgment, understanding or behavioural control.",
